uld have expected that, if there was a bottom dropping out, she`d be among the top, because they went very hard on crime. And she`s performing well in a D-plus-8 district. So I think if it holds there, to the point that Amy`s making, it suggests that the red wave of hoping of taking over all these suburbs isn`t materializing.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Marc Short, weigh in on Amy`s point about how hard it is to make -- to flip some of these districts where Biden ran well and also it -- coming back to your point earlier, candidates matter.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: Candidates matter a lot.\par \par \uc2 I do think that -- I do think Kevin McCarthy did a better job recruiting strong candidates. And I think you`re seeing some of those House pickups, but, to Amy`s point, you`re probably seeing Republicans winning in seats that perhaps Biden won by five, but not stretching it to 10...\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT: ... which would be kind of the red wave of `94 or 2010 you would need.\par \par \uc2 Still, it`s a strong -- it`s a very strong night. I think it`s -- again, the issues were on the Republicans` side. But candidate recruitment matters a lot.\par \par \uc2 PERRY BACON JR: rMD-IN_ And who didn`t recruit good candidates, I think as Marc was hinting at, is Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 It looks like J.D. Vance and Herschel Walker are underperforming the rest of the party in those states. And J.D. Vance probably looks like he will win. Herschel Walker, maybe he wins too. But it looks like, if you -- if Donald Trump was not getting his thumb in the races and allowing Mitch McConnell, a much savvier person about politics, to pick the candidates, I think the Republicans are -- I think you may look at tonight -- Mastriano won, another -- also I think backed by Trump as well.\par \par \uc2 So, I -- that won the primary, I should say.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 P